  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>During an electrophilic addition reactions double bonds donate lone pair electrons to an electrophile.</p><p>Markovnikov's Rule: The addition of a proton <span style=\"background-color:null;\">acid</span> to the double bond of an alkene results in a product with the acid hydrogen bound to the carbon atom that already has the greater number of hydrogen attached.</p><p>In the given reaction electrophile <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> adds to the double bond, a carbocation is formed. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> is added at the electron deficient carbocation.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0050_ffb0b3bc22af.png\" style=\"width: 767px; height: 207px;\"/></p>"
  }
